Efficiency corrections obtained by using the embedding procedure described here have been used to correct the charged hadron spectra in Au+Au collisions and in d+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV, presented in [21]. The charged hadron spectra show good agreement with the results from two other RHIC experiments, BRAHMS and PHOBOS. This demonstrates that the embedding procedure provides a good description of effects influencing the track reconstruction efficiency.

#### Momentum Resolution

The momentum resolution \(\Delta p_{t}\) of the detectors is defined here as the width \(\sigma\) of a gaussian fit to the distribution of the difference of reconstructed and simulated \(p_{t}\) divided by the simulated \(p_{t}\),

\[\Delta p_{t}=\sigma\left(\frac{p_{t,reco}-p_{t,mc}}{p_{t,mc}}\right).\]

Figure 4.6 shows the \(p_{t}\) resolution obtained from embedding as a function of pseudorapidity and transverse momentum for primary tracks, where the event vertex is included in the fit. Tracks from FTPC West with \(2.8<\eta<3.8\) and \(p_{t}<2\) GeV/c are used. The resolution deteriorates with increasing pseudorapidity and increasing \(p_{t}\). Since these one dimensional plots average over the other coordinate (either

